    "ex-qftx-gaga-analytic-hyperplane-quotient": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qftx-analytic-coherent-module",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal O_X)$ be a ringed space, so $X$ is a topological space and $\\mathcal O_X$ is a sheaf of commutative rings. An $\\mathcal O_X$-module sheaf $\\mathcal F$ is **locally of finite type** if for every $x\\in X$ there are an open neighborhood $U$ of $x$, a finite integer $r\\ge0$, and sections $s_1,\\ldots,s_r\\in\\mathcal F(U)$ such that their germs generate $\\mathcal F_y$ as an $\\mathcal O_{X,y}$-module for every $y\\in U$.\n\nFor an open $V\\subseteq X$, a finite integer $p\\ge0$, and an $\\mathcal O_X$-module morphism $\\phi:\\mathcal O_V^{\\oplus p}\\to\\mathcal F|_V$, its **relation sheaf** is the kernel sheaf $\\ker\\phi$. The module sheaf $\\mathcal F$ is **coherent** if it is locally of finite type and, for every such $V$, $p$, and $\\phi$, the relation sheaf $\\ker\\phi$ is locally of finite type. Here $\\mathcal O_V^{\\oplus0}$ is the zero module sheaf.\n\nIn particular, for a finite tuple $f_1,\\ldots,f_p\\in\\mathcal O_X(U)$, its holomorphic relation sheaf is the kernel of the morphism $\\mathcal O_U^{\\oplus p}\\to\\mathcal O_U$ that sends $(a_1,\\ldots,a_p)$ to $\\sum_{j=1}^p a_jf_j$. The structure sheaf is coherent when it is coherent as a module over itself. If $M$ is a complex manifold, an **analytic coherent module on $M$** means a coherent module sheaf over its sheaf $\\mathcal O_M$ of holomorphic functions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-qftx-gaga-analytic-oka-coherence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $M$ be a finite-dimensional complex manifold and let $\\mathcal O_M$ be its sheaf of holomorphic functions. For every open $U\\subseteq M$ and every finite tuple $f_1,\\ldots,f_q\\in\\mathcal O_M(U)$, the relation sheaf\n$$\n\\ker\\!\\left(\\mathcal O_U^{\\oplus q}\\longrightarrow\\mathcal O_U,\\quad(a_1,\\ldots,a_q)\\longmapsto\\sum_{j=1}^q a_jf_j\\right)\n$$\nis locally of finite type: every point has a neighborhood on which one finite family of relation sections generates the relation stalk at every point. Consequently $\\mathcal O_M$ is coherent. Coherent $\\mathcal O_M$-module sheaves are closed under kernels, cokernels, and finite extensions. For every coherent $\\mathcal O_M$-module $\\mathcal F$, point $x\\in M$, and finite integer $\\ell\\ge0$, there are a chart neighborhood $W$ of $x$ and finite ranks $p_0,\\ldots,p_\\ell$ for which\n$$\n\\mathcal O_W^{\\oplus p_\\ell}\\longrightarrow\\cdots\\longrightarrow\\mathcal O_W^{\\oplus p_0}\\longrightarrow\\mathcal F|_W\\longrightarrow0\n$$\nis exact at $\\mathcal F|_W$ and at every free term except the leftmost one. No exactness at the leftmost free term and no global analytic free resolution are asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-direct-image-sheaf",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $f:X\\to Y$ be a continuous map, and let $\\mathcal F$ be a presheaf on $X$.\nThe **direct image presheaf**\n$$f_*\\mathcal F$$\non $Y$ is defined on an open set $V \\subseteq Y$ by\n$$\n(f_*\\mathcal F)(V):=\\mathcal F(f^{-1}(V)).\n$$\nIf $V' \\subseteq V$, the restriction map\n$$\n(f_*\\mathcal F)(V)\\longrightarrow(f_*\\mathcal F)(V')\n$$\nis the restriction map of $\\mathcal F$ for the inclusion\n$f^{-1}(V') \\subseteq f^{-1}(V)$.\n\nThus direct image is just precomposition of the presheaf with the inverse-image\nmap on open sets.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-direct-image-is-sheaf",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:X\\to Y$ be a continuous map.\n\n1. If $\\mathcal F$ is a sheaf on $X$, then $f_*\\mathcal F$ is a sheaf on $Y$.\n2. If $\\mathcal F$ is a sheaf of groups, rings, or modules on $X$, then\n   $f_*\\mathcal F$ is a sheaf of the same kind on $Y$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-sheaf-morphism-isomorphism-stalkwise",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\varphi:\\mathcal F\\to\\mathcal G$ be a morphism of sheaves of sets on a\ntopological space $X$. Then $\\varphi$ is an isomorphism if and only if, for\nevery $x\\in X$, the induced map on stalks\n$$\\varphi_x:\\mathcal F_x\\longrightarrow\\mathcal G_x$$\nis a bijection.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-power-series-expansion-in-several-complex-variables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Fix $m\\ge1$, $a\\in\\mathbb C^m$ and a polyradius $\\rho$, let\n$f:\\Delta_\\rho(a)\\to\\mathbb C$ be continuous and separately holomorphic, let $r$\nbe a polyradius with $r_k<\\rho_k$ for every $k<m$, and let\n$C_k(t)=a_k+r_k\\exp(it)$ on $[0,2\\pi]$. For each multi-index $\\alpha$ set\n\n$$c_\\alpha:=\\frac1{(2\\pi i)^m}\\int_{C_0}\\!\\cdots\\!\\int_{C_{m-1}} f(\\zeta)\\prod_{k<m}(\\zeta_k-a_k)^{-\\alpha_k-1}\\,d\\zeta_{m-1}\\cdots d\\zeta_0 ,$$\n\nan iterated integral as in the polydisc Cauchy formula. Then, with\n$M=\\sup_{\\Gamma_r(a)}|f|$,\n\n$$|c_\\alpha|\\le M\\prod_{k<m}r_k^{-\\alpha_k},$$\n\nand for every real $\\theta$ with $0<\\theta<1$ the series\n$\\sum_\\alpha c_\\alpha(z-a)^\\alpha$ converges absolutely and uniformly on\n$\\overline\\Delta_{\\theta r}(a)$ with\n\n$$f(z)=\\sum_\\alpha c_\\alpha(z-a)^\\alpha\\qquad(z\\in\\overline\\Delta_{\\theta r}(a)).$$\n\nSince every $z\\in\\Delta_r(a)$ lies in $\\overline\\Delta_{\\theta r}(a)$ for some\n$\\theta<1$, the expansion holds throughout $\\Delta_r(a)$.\n\n**The coefficients are asserted here only as those iterated integrals.** That\n$c_\\alpha$ equals $\\partial^\\alpha f(a)/\\alpha!$ needs termwise differentiation\nand is not claimed by this statement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "cor-uniqueness-of-multivariable-power-series-coefficients",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m\\ge1$, $a\\in\\mathbb C^m$ and let $r$ be a polyradius. Suppose\n$c,c':\\mathbb N^m\\to\\mathbb C$ both satisfy a bound\n$|c_\\alpha|\\le M\\prod_{k<m}r_k^{-\\alpha_k}$ and\n$|c'_\\alpha|\\le M'\\prod_{k<m}r_k^{-\\alpha_k}$, and suppose\n\n$$\\sum_\\alpha c_\\alpha(z-a)^\\alpha=\\sum_\\alpha c'_\\alpha(z-a)^\\alpha \\qquad\\text{for every }z\\in\\Delta_r(a).$$\n\nThen $c_\\alpha=c'_\\alpha$ for every multi-index $\\alpha$. In particular a\nfunction has at most one such power-series representation about a given centre,\nand its coefficients are $\\partial^\\alpha_zf(a)/\\alpha!$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-dolbeault-lemma-polydisc",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $n\\ge1$, let $P=\\prod_{j=1}^n D_j$ and\n$P'=\\prod_{j=1}^n D'_j$ be finite open coordinate polydiscs such that each\nclosed coordinate disc $\\overline{D'_j}$ is a compact subset of $D_j$. Let\n$0\\le p\\le n$ and $1\\le q\\le n$, and let $\\eta\\in\\Omega^{p,q}(P)$ be a\nsmooth form with $\\bar\\partial\\eta=0$. Then there is a smooth\n$\\psi\\in\\Omega^{p,q-1}(P')$ such that\n$$\\bar\\partial\\psi=\\eta|_{P'}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-smooth-function-module-sheaves-are-acyclic",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $M$ be a smooth manifold\n([[def-smooth-manifold]]), let\n$\\mathcal A=\\mathcal C_M^\\infty$ be its sheaf of real-valued smooth functions,\nand let $\\mathcal F$ be a sheaf of $\\mathcal A$-modules. Then for every open\n$U\\subseteq M$ and every $q>0$,\n$$H^q(U,\\mathcal F|_U)=0.$$\nThe Axiom of Choice supplies injective resolutions for module sheaves and for\nabelian-sheaf cohomology. Its consequences $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ and DC supply,\nrespectively, the partitions of unity and the acyclic-resolution comparison\nused below; no stronger choice principle is used.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-acyclic-resolution-theorem-for-right-derived-functors",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Dependent Choice.\n\nLet $I$ be a supplied injective resolution datum on a class $\\mathcal D$, let\n$F:\\mathcal A\\to\\mathcal B$ be an additive left exact functor, and let\n$$0\\to A\\to J^0\\to J^1\\to J^2\\to\\cdots$$\nbe an $F$-acyclic resolution of $A$ relative to $I$. Assume moreover that\n$A\\in\\mathcal D$ and that, for $Z^0:=A$ and\n$$0\\to Z^q\\to J^q\\to Z^{q+1}\\to0\\qquad(q\\ge0),$$\neach $Z^q$ lies in $\\mathcal D$. Then for every $n\\ge 0$ there is a canonical\nisomorphism\n$$R_I^nF(A)\\xrightarrow{\\ \\sim\\ }H^n(F(J^\\bullet_{\\mathrm{del}})).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-dolbeault-cohomology-polydisc-vanishes-positive-q",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the full axiom of choice. Let $n\\ge1$ and let\n$P=\\prod_{j=1}^n D_j\\subseteq\\mathbb C^n$, where each $D_j$ is a nonempty\nopen disc of finite positive radius or is $\\mathbb C$. For integers\n$0\\le p\\le n$ and $1\\le q\\le n$, every smooth\n$\\eta\\in\\Omega^{p,q}(P)$ with $\\bar\\partial\\eta=0$ is $\\bar\\partial$-exact:\nthere is a smooth $\\omega\\in\\Omega^{p,q-1}(P)$ such that\n$\\bar\\partial\\omega=\\eta$. Consequently,\n$H_{\\bar\\partial}^{p,q}(P)=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "thm-exactness-of-sheaves-stalkwise",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let\n$$\n\\cdots\\longrightarrow \\mathcal F_{i-1}\\xrightarrow{d_{i-1}}\\mathcal F_i\\xrightarrow{d_i}\\mathcal F_{i+1}\\longrightarrow\\cdots\n$$\nbe a sequence of sheaves of abelian groups on a topological space $X$. Then the\nsequence is exact if and only if, for every point $x\\in X$, the stalk sequence\n$$\n\\cdots\\longrightarrow (\\mathcal F_{i-1})_x\\xrightarrow{(d_{i-1})_x}(\\mathcal F_i)_x\\xrightarrow{(d_i)_x}(\\mathcal F_{i+1})_x\\longrightarrow\\cdots\n$$\nis exact.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "thm-long-exact-sequence-sheaf-cohomology",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let\n$$0\\to\\mathcal F'\\to\\mathcal F\\to\\mathcal F''\\to0$$\nbe a short exact sequence of abelian sheaves on a topological space $X$, and let\n$H^q(X,-)$ be sheaf cohomology computed from the supplied functorial injective\nresolution datum $I$ on $\\mathrm{Ab}(X)$\n([[def-sheaf-cohomology-derived-global-sections]]). Then there is a natural long\nexact sequence\n$$\\cdots\\to H^q(X,\\mathcal F')\\to H^q(X,\\mathcal F)\\to H^q(X,\\mathcal F'')\\xrightarrow{\\partial^q}H^{q+1}(X,\\mathcal F')\\to H^{q+1}(X,\\mathcal F)\\to\\cdots,$$\nnatural in the short exact sequence and independent of the choice of injective\nresolutions used to assemble it.\n\nThe derived categories and right derived functor here are taken under the\nstanding smallness or supplied cofinal-denominator hypothesis of\n[[def-derived-category-of-an-abelian-category]].",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "def-sheaf-cohomology-derived-global-sections",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Fix a topological space $X$, let\n$$\\Gamma(X,-):\\mathrm{Ab}(X)\\to\\mathbf{Ab}$$\nbe the additive left exact global-sections functor\n([[def-global-sections-functor-sheaves]]), and let $I$ be the supplied\nfunctorial injective resolution datum on $\\mathrm{Ab}(X)$ of\n[[thm-abelian-sheaves-have-enough-injectives]]: it assigns to every abelian\nsheaf $\\mathcal F$ one specific injective resolution\n$0\\to\\mathcal F\\to I^\\bullet(\\mathcal F)$\n([[def-right-derived-object-relative-to-injective-resolution-data]]).\n\nFor every $q\\ge0$ and every abelian sheaf $\\mathcal F$ on $X$ define the\n**$q$-th sheaf cohomology group of $\\mathcal F$** to be the right derived\nobject of $\\Gamma(X,-)$ relative to $I$:\n$$H^q(X,\\mathcal F):=R_I^q\\Gamma(X,\\mathcal F)=H^q\\bigl(\\Gamma(X,I^\\bullet(\\mathcal F)_{\\mathrm{del}})\\bigr),$$\nthe cohomology object of the complex\n$\\Gamma(X,I^0(\\mathcal F))\\to\\Gamma(X,I^1(\\mathcal F))\\to\\cdots$ of abelian\ngroups obtained from the deleted resolution\n([[def-deleted-resolution]], [[def-cohomology-object-of-a-cochain-complex]]).\nWe also set $H^q(X,\\mathcal F):=0$ for $q<0$.\n\nBecause $I$ is a fixed supplied datum, the group $H^q(X,\\mathcal F)$ is a\nspecific group for each pair $(X,\\mathcal F)$, and for a morphism\n$\\varphi:\\mathcal F\\to\\mathcal G$ of abelian sheaves we write\n$H^q(X,\\varphi):=R_I^q\\Gamma(X,\\varphi)$ for the induced map; it is the map on\ncohomology induced by $\\Gamma(X,-)$ applied to the cochain maps supplied with\nthe datum, and it is additive in $\\varphi$.\n\nIf $J$ is another supplied injective resolution datum on $\\mathrm{Ab}(X)$ then\n$R_I^q\\Gamma(X,-)$ and $R_J^q\\Gamma(X,-)$ are naturally isomorphic, so the\n$H^q(X,\\mathcal F)$ computed from $J$ agree with the ones above up to a\ncanonical natural isomorphism; the Axiom of Dependent Choice needed for that\ncomparison follows from AC\n([[thm-right-derived-functors-from-two-supplied-injective-resolution-data-are-naturally-isomorphic]],\n[[thm-choice-implies-dependent-implies-countable-choice]]). The notation\n$H^q(X,\\mathcal F)$ therefore does not depend on the datum used, up to this\ncanonical isomorphism, and we call $q$ the **cohomological degree**.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "On $D$, multiplication by $z_1$ gives a morphism $\\mathcal O_D\\to\\mathcal O_D$. Restriction of holomorphic functions to $H_D$ is a morphism $\\mathcal O_D\\to i_*\\mathcal O_{H_D}$ by [F3]–[F4], and it kills the image of multiplication by $z_1$. It therefore induces a morphism $\\bar r:\\mathcal Q\\to i_*\\mathcal O_{H_D}$. The quotient is coherent by [F1] and closure under cokernels in [F2].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "At a point $a=(0,a')\\in H_D$, a germ $h$ holomorphic on $H_D$ extends to $\\widetilde h(z_1,z')=h(z')$, so $\\bar r_a$ is surjective by [F3]. If $f$ is a holomorphic germ at $a$ whose restriction to $z_1=0$ vanishes, write its convergent expansion on a smaller product polydisc as $f(z_1,a'+\\zeta')=\\sum_{k,\\alpha'}c_{k,\\alpha'}z_1^k(\\zeta')^{\\alpha'}$. Since the restriction is the zero germ, [F6] implies $c_{0,\\alpha'}=0$ for every $\\alpha'$. The series $g(z_1,a'+\\zeta')=\\sum_{k\\ge1,\\alpha'}c_{k,\\alpha'}z_1^{k-1}(\\zeta')^{\\alpha'}$ still converges on every smaller product polydisc, because shifting each positive $z_1$ exponent down by one preserves the positive radius of convergence. Thus $g$ is holomorphic and $f=z_1g$. The kernel of restriction on this stalk is exactly $(z_1)$, so $\\bar r_a$ is an isomorphism.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "On this arbitrary $D$, [F7] gives the exact smooth Dolbeault sheaf resolution of $\\mathcal O_D$. Its terms are modules over smooth real-valued functions; [F8] makes them acyclic and identifies $H^q(D,\\mathcal O_D)$ with the cohomology of their global complex. By [F9] that complex has zero cohomology for $1\\le q\\le N$, and its terms are zero above degree $N$. Hence $H^q(D,\\mathcal O_D)=0$ for every $q>0$.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "If $a\\in D\\setminus H_D$, then $z_1(a)\\ne0$, so the germ of $z_1$ is a unit and $\\mathcal Q_a=0$. The stalk $(i_*\\mathcal O_{H_D})_a$ is zero by [F3]. Together with step 1.2 this proves that $\\bar r$ is an isomorphism on every stalk; [F5] gives the sheaf isomorphism.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "step 1.2",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Multiplication by $z_1$ is injective on every holomorphic stalk. Away from $H_D$ it multiplies by a unit. At $a=(0,a')\\in H_D$, if $z_1f=0$ as a germ, expand $f$ on a sufficiently small product polydisc about $a$ using [F6]. Multiplication shifts every $z_1$ exponent up by one; uniqueness of the power-series coefficients therefore forces all coefficients of $f$ to vanish. Thus $f=0$. By [F10] multiplication is a sheaf monomorphism; its cokernel is $\\mathcal Q$ by definition, giving the stated short exact sequence on the whole $D$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "F10",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Apply [F11] to the short exact sequence of step 2.2. For $q>0$, its segment $H^q(D,\\mathcal O_D)\\to H^q(D,\\mathcal Q)\\to H^{q+1}(D,\\mathcal O_D)$ has both outer groups zero by step 1.3, so $H^q(D,\\mathcal Q)=0$. Its degree-zero segment also makes $\\mathcal O(D)\\to\\Gamma(D,\\mathcal Q)$ surjective with kernel $z_1\\mathcal O(D)$, because $H^1(D,\\mathcal O_D)=0$. Finally the sheaf isomorphism of step 2.1 and the direct-image formula [F3] give $\\Gamma(D,\\mathcal Q)=\\Gamma(D,i_*\\mathcal O_{H_D})=\\mathcal O(H_D)$. Thus both the global quotient computation and all positive-degree vanishings hold for every coordinate polydisc containing $0$, using only scalar acyclicity and this multiplication sequence.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F11",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "step 1.3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
